---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import LearningCurve from '../components/LearningCurve.astro';
import { loadLeaderboard, loadLearningCurves } from '../lib/selfplay';

const boards = loadLeaderboard();
const curves = loadLearningCurves();

// headline numbers pulled from the actual run data
const demoFinals = curves
  .filter((s) => s.run === 'demo')
  .map((s) => {
    const tail = s.points.slice(-20);
    return {
      puzzle: s.puzzle,
      final: tail[tail.length - 1]!.validity,
      mean: tail.reduce((a, p) => a + p.validity, 0) / tail.length,
    };
  });
const controlMax = Math.max(
  ...curves.filter((s) => s.run === 'random-control').flatMap((s) => s.points.map((p) => p.validity)),
);
---

<Base
  title="Self-play"
  description="Three interlocking self-play loops, the expert-iteration learning curve, and the self-competition leaderboard."
>
  <div class="eyebrow">Verifier-in-the-loop program synthesis</div>
  <h1>Self-play</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Opus Magnum has an exact, cheap simulator that says with certainty whether a
    machine works and what it scores — which makes self-play viable with
    <strong>zero external solution data</strong>. External puzzles are allowed;
    external <em>solutions</em> are never training data. Everything below derives from
    <code>docs/self-play-design.md</code>, the expert-iteration run logs, and the
    incumbent store. <a href="/docs/self-play-design/">Read the full design →</a>
  </p>

  <h2>The three loops</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Loop 1 · Solver-as-teacher</h3>
      <p>
        <em>Expert iteration</em> (ExIt / AlphaZero / AlphaDev): a fast learned
        proposer drafts a machine; the repo's exact solvers (clingo/SAT/Picat) improve
        the draft; the improved plan — verified by the canonical validator — becomes
        the training target. The solver is the bootstrap expert, with a known ceiling
        around 2 arms / ~36 steps.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Loop 2 · Forward-generation curriculum</h3>
      <p>
        Run random machines <em>forward</em> to manufacture guaranteed-solvable
        puzzles with known reference solutions, from game rules alone. A generator
        co-evolves POET/PAIRED-style to keep puzzles at the edge of current
        competence, with coverage quotas and a fixed anchor set to prevent
        distribution collapse.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Loop 3 · Self-competition</h3>
      <p>
        A private leaderboard keyed by (puzzle, metric) where the system races its own
        best verified solution — reward is beating your own record. External records
        live in a read-only comparison column that no training code can read as a
        target; the constraint is enforced by schema, not policy.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Learning curve — expert iteration vs random control</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    In the demo run the proposer's <strong>proposal validity</strong> (share of drafts
    that pass the canonical validator, 10-iteration rolling window) climbs from 0 to
    {demoFinals.map((d) => ` ${d.final.toFixed(2)} on ${d.puzzle} (last-20 mean ${d.mean.toFixed(2)})`).join(' and')}.
    The random-control run never gets off the floor — its window validity peaks at
    {controlMax.toFixed(2)} and ends at 0.00 on both puzzles.
  </p>
  <LearningCurve />
  <details>
    <summary>Table view of the same data</summary>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>run</th>
            <th>puzzle</th>
            <th>iterations</th>
            <th>final window validity</th>
            <th>peak window validity</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {curves.map((s) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{s.run}</td>
              <td><code>{s.puzzle}</code></td>
              <td>{s.points.length}</td>
              <td>{s.points[s.points.length - 1]!.validity.toFixed(2)}</td>
              <td>{Math.max(...s.points.map((p) => p.validity)).toFixed(2)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>Self-competition leaderboard</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Every incumbent below is one of the system's <strong>own</strong> solutions,
    verified by <code>harness/validate.py</code> at submission time — loaded from
    <code>selfplay/leaderboard/incumbents.json</code>. The external comparison column
    is empty so far: no external records have been imported, and none would ever be a
    training target.
  </p>
  {boards.map((board) => (
    <section>
      <h3><code>{board.puzzle}</code></h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>metric</th>
              <th>our best</th>
              <th>source</th>
              <th>submission #</th>
              <th>external best (read-only)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {board.cells.map((c) => (
              <tr>
                <td><code>{c.metric}</code></td>
                <td>{c.score}</td>
                <td>{c.source}</td>
                <td>{c.submission}</td>
                <td>—</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}
  <p class="section-note">
    Stubbed metrics (not yet computed, not on the board): area_at_infinity, looping,
    rate.
  </p>
</Base>
